{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "1",
    "case_summary": "A 62-year-old woman with a history of metabolic syndrome presented with chest pain persisting for four months, without any increase in frequency or duration. She reported no pain at rest, nocturnal pain, difficulty breathing, or palpitations. An echocardiographic stress examination indicated significant ischemia with anteroseptal hypokinesia, while cardiac chambers appeared morphologically normal. Coronary angiography identified stenosis in the proximal left anterior descending coronary artery (LAD) near the left main stem. A stent was placed in the proximal LAD, resulting in no residual stenosis. The ventricle was morphologically normal. Follow-up coronary angiography 13 months post-stent implantation showed no re-stenosis and a normal left ventricle. No echocardiogram was conducted at this follow-up.\n\nA transthoracic echocardiography disclosed a big (70 × 30 mm) mass in the left atrium attached to the interatrial septum. The tumor prolapsed into the left ventricle obstructing the mitral valve orifice. The mean pressure gradient across the mitral valve was 8 mm Hg.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
